+# Resume Profiles
+
+- Save profiles in `resume-profiles.json` directly under your Codex home directory.
+- Profiles are available for local sessions, including the local daemon, and are not loaded or offered when using a remote filesystem.
+- Use an object keyed by single digits from `0` through `9`.
+- Each profile requires a `name` containing one nonempty word without whitespace or control characters.
+- Entries with a missing, non-string, empty or multiword name are skipped.
+- `name` labels the shortcut and does not change the session title.
+- Each profile can set `cwd`, `model`, `reasoning_effort`, `title`, and `archive`.
+- Omitted model, effort, directory and title fields preserve the session's current values.
+- `archive` defaults to `false`, leaving the session unarchived after application.
+- Set `archive` to `true` to archive after the other profile values have been saved.
+- A named profile containing only `archive` as an action is valid.
+- `cwd` must be an absolute local path.
+- `model` must be available from the session's existing provider.
+- `reasoning_effort` uses the same values as the ordinary model settings.
+- A supplied `title` replaces the current name and must not be blank.
+- The example configuration is in [resume-profiles.json](/harness/repos/codex/main-worktree/source/docs/examples/resume-profiles.json).
+
+# Applying A Profile
+
+- In `codex resume`, select an active or archived session and press its configured `Alt+0` through `Alt+9` shortcut.
+- The footer lists configured digits in ascending order on a separate line, fitting multiple shortcuts before wrapping at the available width.
+- Each shortcut shows its profile name, such as `alt+1 apply profile subora`.
+- During application, the profile shortcut rows show only `saving to profile <name>` for the selected profile.
+- The ordinary profile shortcuts return after success or failure.
+- Profile names use distinct readable foreground colors from the active theme when available.
+- Slot colors remain stable when other slots are absent.
+- Themes with fewer than ten distinct readable colors reuse their available colors.
+- Configured shortcut hints are loaded when the picker opens.
+- The selected profile is reread from disk for each application.
+- The picker applies the profile immediately and stays open.
+- Profile application uses the existing thread settings, rename, archive and restore APIs.
+- Model, effort or directory changes attach to the thread only while applying settings.
+- Applying settings or a title to an archived session first restores it because those native APIs require an unarchived thread.
+- A title-only or archive-only profile does not attach to the thread.
+- It submits no user message and starts no model turn.
+- Settings are saved using the existing thread-settings checkpoint in the session rollout.
+- Settings completion is acknowledged even when the requested values already match, including after a fresh process restores the session.
+- Names use the existing thread-name persistence mechanism.
+- Already applied restores, settings and names remain applied if a later operation fails.
+- The picker updates the changed session in place, preserving selection, filters and pagination without rereading unrelated sessions.
+- Native Archive still archives spawned descendants, which are excluded from the picker's session sources.
+- Errors from profile application or targeted metadata reads remain visible in the picker.
+- Archiving the currently open conversation remains restricted to `/archive`.
+- This does not change global model defaults or permissions profiles.
